\section{Covariance estimation}{

Let \code{Z} contain \code{n} observations in rows. The function returns the projection
of the supplied \code{S} without changing its normalization:
\itemize{
\item If the mean is known to be zero and \code{S = crossprod(Z) / n}, the projection
is the covariance MLE in the specified symmetry model, when that MLE exists.
\item If \code{S = cov(Z)}, the projection retains the denominator \code{n - 1} used by
\code{\link[stats:cov]{stats::cov()}}. For the full Gaussian likelihood maximized over both an
unrestricted mean and a covariance matrix with the specified symmetry,
the covariance MLE would be \code{(n - 1) / n * project_matrix(S, perm)}, when it exists.
}

For \code{S = cov(Z)} with \code{was_mean_estimated = TRUE}, \code{gips} uses the projection
without this rescaling, and \code{\link[=logLik.gips]{logLik.gips()}} uses \code{n - 1} in its likelihood
calculation. This is the package's convention based on centered data,
distinct from the full Gaussian likelihood described above. See the
\strong{Estimated mean} subsection in \code{vignette("Theory", package = "gips")}.
}

\section{MLE availability}{

\code{project_matrix()} does not receive the sample size and does not check
whether an MLE is available. It can return a singular projected matrix.
For a corresponding \code{gips} object \code{g}, \code{\link[=summary.gips]{summary.gips()}} reports the
sample-size threshold in \code{summary(g)$n0}. The field \code{summary(g)$MLE_available}
combines the sample-size check with a numerical positive-definiteness check
of the projection, using the package's convention described above.
}

\section{Multi-sample}{

When \code{S} is a list of matrices (as stored in a multi-sample \code{gips} object),
\code{project_matrix()} applies the same permutation projection to each element
and returns a list of projected matrices of the same length.
}

\examples{
p <- 6
my_perm <- "(14)(23)" # permutation (1,4)(2,3)(5)(6)
number_of_observations <- 10
X <- matrix(rnorm(p * number_of_observations), number_of_observations, p)
S <- cov(X)
projected_S <- project_matrix(S, perm = my_perm)
projected_S
# The value in [1,1] is the same as in [4,4]; also, [2,2] and [3,3];
  # also [1,2] and [3,4]; also, [1,5] and [4,5]; and so on

# Plot the projected matrix:
g <- gips(S, number_of_observations, perm = my_perm)
plot(g, type = "MLE")

# Search for the MAP symmetry group:
g_MAP <- find_MAP(g, max_iter = 10, show_progress_bar = FALSE, optimizer = "Metropolis_Hastings")
# Then, project S onto the selected model:
S_MAP <- project_matrix(attr(g, "S"), perm = g_MAP)
S_MAP
plot(g_MAP, type = "heatmap")
}
